# rules: java-exception-detail-reaches-the-client
# languages: java
# fixture: java/CWE-209.java
# from: CWE-209 (Generation of Error Message Containing Sensitive Information), CWE-497
#
# A Java exception message is written for whoever runs the program, not for
# whoever called it. It carries absolute paths, class and method names, the
# SQL that failed with its table and column names, and frequently the value
# that failed to parse. Sending it to the client turns every malformed request
# into a free map of the application, and it is free because the program
# volunteered it.
#
# What separates this from careful code is the *destination*, and that is the
# one thing here that is syntax. A logger and `System.out` are where an
# exception's detail belongs and both are ordinary inside a catch, so the rule
# names the response explicitly - `getWriter`, `getOutputStream`, `sendError`
# - and reports nothing else. `sendError(code)` with no message is the fix and
# has a different arity, so it is left alone by construction.
#
# The exception is recognised by what is asked of it rather than by its type,
# because a catch parameter's type is whatever the method throws and the rule
# would have to know every one of them. `getMessage`, `toString`,
# `getLocalizedMessage`, `getStackTrace` and `getCause` are the five ways to
# ask an exception what went wrong, and they are what the guard tests for.
#
# The writer taken into a local first - `PrintWriter w = response.getWriter()`
# and then `w.println(...)` further down - is a second reading of the same
# code, with the two holes required to be the same name. It has to be a
# sibling of the write to be seen, which is the usual limit of a two-statement
# pattern - a writer taken before a `try` and written inside the `catch` is in
# a different statement list and is not reported. The shape that does read as
# siblings is Spring's @ExceptionHandler, which is handed the exception rather
# than catching it, and that is the common one.

( "getMessage\\(\\)|getLocalizedMessage\\(\\)|getStackTrace\\(\\)|"
  + "getCause\\(\\)|getSQLState\\(\\)|getErrorCode\\(\\)|"
  + "(?i)\\b(e|ex|exc|exce?ption|err|error|t|th|throwable|cause)[0-9]?"
  + "\\.toString\\(\\)" ) as $detail
| ["$R.getWriter().print($M)", "$R.getWriter().println($M)",
   "$R.getWriter().write($M)", "$R.getWriter().append($M)",
   "$R.getOutputStream().print($M)", "$R.getOutputStream().println($M)",
   "$R.getOutputStream().write($M)",
   "$R.sendError($_, $M)"] as $toResponse
| ["PrintWriter $W = $R.getWriter();\n$$$_\n$W.print($M);",
   "PrintWriter $W = $R.getWriter();\n$$$_\n$W.println($M);",
   "PrintWriter $W = $R.getWriter();\n$$$_\n$W.write($M);"] as $throughALocal
| ["$E.printStackTrace($R.getWriter())",
   "$E.printStackTrace($R.getOutputStream())"] as $traces
| scan_ast("*.java"; $toResponse + $throughALocal + $traces) as $all
| ( ($all | of($toResponse) | where_capture("M"; $detail) | focus("M"))
    + ($all | of($throughALocal) | where_capture("M"; $detail) | focus("M"))
    + ($all | of($traces) | focus("E")) )
| finding("java-exception-detail-reaches-the-client";
    "this sends the exception's own account of what went wrong to whoever "
    + "caused it - paths, class names and the failing value included")
| report
